# Tasks

**An index.** One row per task: status, owner, priority, and a one-line hook
pointing at [`{{TASKS_DIR}}/<id>-<slug>.md`]({{TASKS_DIR}}/), where the
description, the constraints and everything written while doing the work live.
Same shape as the findings, prior-work and claims indexes.

| ID    | Title                          | Status            | Priority | Owner | Notes |
|-------|--------------------------------|-------------------|----------|-------|-------|
| T-001 | Example: define the first task | {{STATUS_READY}}  | P1       |       | Replace this row with a real task. [→ doc]({{TASKS_DIR}}/T-001-example.md) |

**Never hand-edit this table.** `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks add` creates a row and
its doc together; `tasks set` moves status and owner; `tasks note <id>` appends
to the doc, which is where prose goes.

## Status values

| Value | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `backlog` | Known but not yet ready to work — needs scoping or prerequisites |
| `{{STATUS_READY}}` | Fully specified; safe to claim |
| `{{STATUS_IN_PROGRESS}}` | Claimed by an owner; actively worked |
| `{{STATUS_IN_REVIEW}}` | In PR / awaiting review |
| `{{STATUS_DONE}}` | Landed |

## Priority values

| Value | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `P0` | Must ship next; everything else paused |
| `P1` | Soon — current cycle |
| `P2` | When convenient |
| `P3` | Backlog; revisit when planning the next cycle |

## Adding a task

```bash
{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks add --id T-042 --title "One-line summary" \
    --priority P1 --hook "**Blocked on T-017.**"
```

That writes the row **and** `{{TASKS_DIR}}/T-042-one-line-summary.md`, so they
cannot be created apart. Then open the doc and fill in what the task is for.

- **ID** — short unique identifier. Pick the next number after the highest existing id.
- **Title** — one-line summary; readable in isolation
- **Status** — `backlog` if it needs more scoping, `{{STATUS_READY}}` if it's actionable
- **Priority** — best guess; can be revised
- **Owner** — leave blank until claimed; the `claim` skill sets this
- **Notes** — **a hook and a pointer, never a journal.** One line on what a reader
  needs before deciding whether to open the doc: what blocks it, what it settled,
  what it cost. Everything else goes in the doc.

## Workflow

| Action | What to do |
|---|---|
| Claim a task | Run the `claim` skill (auto-picks the highest-priority `{{STATUS_READY}}` row) or `/claim <id>` |
| Read the task | `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks doc <id> --print` — the row is not the task |
| Write anything down | `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks note <id> --text '…'` |
| Open a PR | `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks set <id> --status {{STATUS_IN_REVIEW}}` |
| PR merges | `{{RECORDS_HELPER}} tasks set <id> --status {{STATUS_DONE}}` |
| Pause work mid-task | Leave Status at `{{STATUS_IN_PROGRESS}}`; say where you left off with `tasks note` |
| Abandon a task | Status to `backlog`, Owner empty, and `tasks note` **why** — the why is the part that saves the next person |

`{{RECORDS_HELPER}} lint tasks` checks that every row points at a doc, that the
doc exists, and that the hook has not grown back into an essay. Wire it into the
test suite: a rule nothing is held to is documentation.
